3.Fine Tune:
    Model that trains personal data
    Update it every day    
Fine-tuning means updating/training the model with new data.
We need to update the model whenever new information is required.
Instead of retraining the whole model, we can maintain private/custom data.
Used when the model must learn domain-specific knowledge.
4.RAG (Retrieval Augmented Generation)
Uses external sources.
Retrieves data before generating a response.
Data sources can be:
PDFs
Audio files
Videos
HTML/Websites
Documents
Databases
Process
User asks a query.
System checks external knowledge sources.
Relevant information is retrieved.
LLM generates an answer using retrieved information.
Fine-Tuning vs RAG
Fine-Tuning
Train the model.
Cost-efficient for repeated specialized tasks.
Knowledge becomes part of the model.
Requires retraining when data changes.
Difficult to maintain frequent updates.
RAG
2 parts:
Data Ingestion Pipeline-Injecting data into vectors
Data Retrieval Pipeline

Give documents directly to the model.
No retraining required.
Easier to maintain and update.
Uses external knowledge sources.
Better when information changes frequently.
LLM (Large Language Model)
Definition

LLM = Large Language Model

Query Flow

User Query → Prompt → LLM → Response

Prompt

Prompt = The instruction/question you give to the model.

Example:

"How are you?"
"Write a Python program."
"Summarize this document."
Internal Working of an LLM
Human Language → Machine Language
User enters text.
Text is converted into tokens.
Tokens are converted into embeddings.
Embeddings are processed by the model.
Model predicts the next token.
Tokens are converted back into text.
Response is generated.
Embeddings:
Embeddings are numerical vector representations of text.
Example:
"What is Python?"
        ↓
Embedding Vector
[0.25, 0.81, 0.42, ...]

Purpose:

Capture semantic meaning.
Used in search, retrieval, and RAG systems.
Tokens

A token is the smallest unit processed by an LLM.

Examples:

"ChatGPT is great"

Tokens:
["Chat", "GPT", "is", "great"]
Frontend

Frontend handles:

User interface
Login pages
Forms
Displaying responses

Example Technologies:

HTML
CSS
JavaScript
React
Backend / Response Generation
Next Token Prediction

LLMs generate text by predicting the most probable next token repeatedly.

Example:

Input: "India is a"

Prediction:
→ country

This process continues until the complete response is generated.

